{
  "dom_id": "step-q109855",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(XY)$ for all compatible matrices $X$ and $Y?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}({Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T})$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}({Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T})$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(XY)$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Col}(XY)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $XY$, that is, the rows of $(XY)^{T}={Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(XY)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $XY,$ which is $\\text{Null}({Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14267617/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
